1:/// Live agent-stack wiring through the builder (issue #1079, slice 2 —
2:/// the CLI converts to this as the first host shell; slice 3 — the
3:/// fabric/subagent/task complex joins the builder-owned set; slice 4 —
4:/// host extensions become builder-gated, declared surface; slice 6 —
5:/// the extension host's RUNTIME behavior is pinned record-identical to
6:/// the CLI host's, `test/hosts/extension_host_parity_test.dart`).
7:///
8:/// [wireAgentCore] consumes a [HostCapabilityProfile] plus the host's
9:/// typed [AgentCoreServices] and returns a [WiredAgentCore]: the
10:/// capability-gated environment chain, the core tool list, and the
11:/// surfaced/hidden plan — from which [WiredAgentCore.buildAgentStack]
12:/// assembles the [ToolRegistry] and the [Agent] itself. This is the
13:/// shared layer the thin host shells construct their stack through:
14:/// the host provides services and callbacks, the builder owns every
15:/// gating decision (what wires, over which transports, what stays
16:/// hidden with its reason).
17:///
18:/// **Run narrowing** (the profile-narrows invariant, applied per run):
19:/// before the plan is built, the profile is narrowed against the
20:/// services the host actually provided — a wired capability whose
21:/// required services are absent turns `off` with an honest reason, and
22:/// wired transports whose per-transport services are absent are dropped
23:/// (a capability narrowed to zero transports turns `off`). The E1 check
24:/// then stays meaningful: whatever REMAINS wired must have its services,
25:/// or the build fails loudly naming the gap. Today's CLI semantics are
26:/// exactly this: a null config section means the capability is off this
27:/// run — now the absence is a declared state with a reason instead of a
28:/// silent hand-rolled `if`.
29:///
30:/// Pure Dart: no `dart:io`.
